ASSEMBLY

The text for this opertaing system uses some ideas from assembly.

Assembly instructions are for the cpu to run 'directly' with the fetch execute cycle.
They are stored directly in RAM. The fetch execute cycle can be described as an infinite loop
with the following iteration:

1. Lookup the instruction at index PC (program counter) in RAM (initially set to 0).
2. Interpret this instruction, and execute it.
3. Move PC up by 4.

Certain instruction break the loop or move the PC elsewhere.

These instructions are usually pretty simple. The cpu can load values into registers from RAM,
add and multiply numbers from registers. There are also instructions to load a value from RAM, or put
a value into RAM.

C programs can be compiled into assembly code, and assembly can be compiled into machine code.
It's interesting that even the most complicated C and C++ programs translate to a sequence of
instructions, each one being very simple.

Going from assembly to machine/binary is easier, there is a standardized way to do it depending on your machine architecture. 
The former has a lot more variation.

XV6 BOOTUP

We give an overview for the bootup process of xv6. This will be vague, more detail will be provided
when we study different parts of the OS further.

- riscv-computer turns on and initializes (perhaps turning on all devices in the computer).

- runs a boot loader program, stored in read-only memory (where might this be stored?)

- the boot loader program loads xv6 into RAM presumably. specifically at physical address 0x800000 because
the lower addresses are reserved for the simulated hardware.

-when xv6 first runs, we make a user stack so that we can run C programs.
Q: is xv6 completely a C program at this point? are we not already a C program?
I need to get a better handle of what running C programs mean. 

- kernel/start.c runs. some more initialization is done here, in particular ones that can be done only 
in machine mode, then we switch to supervisor mode.

-writes main's address into register $mepc

-disable virtual address translation, writing 0 into page-table register $satp

-makes it so that interruptions, exceptions run in supervisor mode ?

-programs clock chip to generate timer interrupts

- Now user programs are ready to run. We call userinit in kernel/proc.c, the first
process. This program calls the system call exec, transforming
the current program into the /init process, the first user level program. This
does some initialization some initialization (console device file? open file descriptors?),
followed by a call to exec to run the shell program user/sh.c . The system is up.

XV6 SYSTEM CALLS

How does a user program make a system call?

Into the shell we type 'trace 32 grep hello README'. This
runs the user program described by user/trace.c. 

We call the user level system call trace with the first of these arguments, 32 in this case.
This turns on a flag corresponding to what statements get traced or printed. After 
setting this flag, we call exec with 'grep hello README'. The statements corresponding to the
flag we set with trace() are printed in the console.

The 'trace()' function that gets called is in user/user.h. It's implementation is different from usual,
it is in usys.S, which gets generated by the Pearl script usys.pl. It is in assmebly, and has the form:

.global trace
trace:
    li a7, SYS_trace
    ecall
    ret

The first line basically makes the 'trace' program accessible from any program that was built with usys.S. We see
that the trace program is only 3 lines of assembly. and here is my guess on what it does.

Into the register a7, we load the system call number for SYS_trace. This macro is defined somewhere in the kernel code.
We jump into the kernel in supervisor mode (trap?) with the intention to perform this system call. This is the usual routine for invoking system calls (what are some details about this process?). 
In this particular case we run the function uint64_t sys_trace(int). `ret` signifies the end of the program. 

The 'sys_' version of the system call is the version that runs in kernel mode. The system call name without
the sys is the one the user calls, which is a 3 line assembly program that loads the system call number and invokes
ecall.

All other system calls are implemented similarly.

Question: In the body of the sys_ version of a system call, how do we get the arguments? It seems that
the kernel implementatiosn for system calls expect it to be in
p->trapframe->a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5.

Where in the code, and along the system call routine, do they put the system call arguments in these registers?


XV6 TRACING

We would like to make it so that for an individual run of a user program that the user chooses, a select number of system 
calls print to the console before finishing. Here is one way to do this.


-Use the proc structure of the child process made when running the user program.Add a new variable to the proc structure called tracing_flags
-This variable is 0 most of the time. The only way to set it is with a call to sys_trace(), which should set the calling process' tracing_flags accordingly.
-When the program that called trace runs execv to transform itself into the user program,it will have the same tracing flags.
-When the user program or any of its children calls sys_fork, make sure to copy to the child process our tracing flags. So that the new process has tracing on.

This is a nice implementation since only the specified user program and its descendants have tracing. It did not require modifying the kernel too much. A messier way to do this might, for example, set a global tracing variable in the kernel and rewrite every other system call.

ATTACK XV6

In this portion of the lab, secret.c writes a string to an arbitrary location in its memory. This portion of the lab asks us to
recover this secret. We assume that the string is 8 bytes or less, including the null character. To recover this secret, in attack.c
we immediately ask for about 32 pages of memory using the sbrk system call, and then print the first 100 bytes or so, so that the user
can manually look for the secret.

If we run attack.c immediately after secret.c, we will see that the secret is always in the same spot, because xv6 distributes pages
in a deterministic manner. So attack.c just returns the 8 byte string in the location we previously determined the secret to be in.

Note that this attack does not work when we run attacktest, probably because the allocation of attacktest changes how pages are distributed,
causing the secret to be in a different location. But I don't want to spend too much time on this portion of the lab, I already have the
basic idea and I want to learn other things about operatin systems.

